QUESTION 1. How Was the data split?
ANSWER:
The split was temporal.

Training Data:
Starts: September 2020
Ends: December 2023
Contains approximately 270-330 records per month

Testing Data:
Starts: January 2024
Ends: September 2024
Contains approximately 266-298 records per month

This split is ideal for our forecasting task because:
We're using older data (2020-2023) to train the model
Testing on the most recent data (2024)
The monthly record distribution is relatively consistent
We have 9 months of test data to validate our 3-month predictions

QUESTION 2. Irene offered to update the code to use 365(1 year sequences). 
Answer:
I advised that, it would cause dimensionality issues since I had only trained the model on 166 features.
The dimensionality issues can be easily solved too, so, anyone can free to experiment with any sequence size, that's why we are working as a team after all. 
I have chosen a 30-day sequence as an easier start for implementation.

QUESTION 3. Are evaluating data on the same 30-days sequence, or is the sequence going through all the months obviously in a 30-day sequence like batches in a way so that we can ensure that we are going through the entire training dataset?
Answer:
Currently, we're using rolling 30-day sequences
The model processes sequences by sliding the 30-day window through all available months
Each sequence overlaps with the previous one, moving forward one day at a time
This ensures we capture patterns across the entire dataset